{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-79047" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=2+3x-{e}^{-x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/state.json" "file_sha256" "9853dfd80c511e06bf8477b89193addcba25bd4fa13ad4df82612d60871edaa2"} {"question" "q-79047" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3+{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-x}>0$ for every real value of $x,$ it follows that $-{e}^{-x}<0$ for every real value of $x.$ Therefore, ${f}^{″}(x)$ is never positive, so there are no intervals where $f(x)$ is concave up.\n\nSince ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ for every real value of $x,$ the function is concave down on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/state.json" "file_sha256" "9853dfd80c511e06bf8477b89193addcba25bd4fa13ad4df82612d60871edaa2"} {"question" "q-79047" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ce03f4f75f1275c5c0ab23092d155e04e8161764ce97c90f3fc6d28c8e8dede"} {"question" "q-79047" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/state.json" "file_sha256" "9853dfd80c511e06bf8477b89193addcba25bd4fa13ad4df82612d60871edaa2"} {"question" "q-79047" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true."]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/state.json" "file_sha256" "9853dfd80c511e06bf8477b89193addcba25bd4fa13ad4df82612d60871edaa2"} {"question" "q-79047" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/state.json" "file_sha256" "9853dfd80c511e06bf8477b89193addcba25bd4fa13ad4df82612d60871edaa2"} {"question" "q-114060" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/assets/c528a8bdbb9bc6dd1a9895109fdc6818b70787c5024c78e06e2d2ec3997777d6.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={f}^{″}(x).$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/state.json" "file_sha256" "9853dfd80c511e06bf8477b89193addcba25bd4fa13ad4df82612d60871edaa2"} {"question" "q-114060" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A function $f(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={f}^{″}(x),$ we can see that ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ on the intervals  $(-1,0)$ and  $(0,1).$  Therefore, the function $f(x)$ is concave up on the intervals  $(-1,0)$ and  $(0,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/state.json" "file_sha256" "9853dfd80c511e06bf8477b89193addcba25bd4fa13ad4df82612d60871edaa2"} {"question" "q-114060" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ce03f4f75f1275c5c0ab23092d155e04e8161764ce97c90f3fc6d28c8e8dede"} {"question" "q-114060" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/state.json" "file_sha256" "9853dfd80c511e06bf8477b89193addcba25bd4fa13ad4df82612d60871edaa2"} {"question" "q-114060" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-\\infty ,0)"] ["math" "(0,1)"] ["math" "(0,\\infty )"] ["text" "$(-1,0)$ and  $(0,1)$"] ["text" "$(-\\infty ,-1)$ and  $(1,\\infty )$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/state.json" "file_sha256" "9853dfd80c511e06bf8477b89193addcba25bd4fa13ad4df82612d60871edaa2"} {"question" "q-114060" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(-1,0)$ and  $(0,1)$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/state.json" "file_sha256" "9853dfd80c511e06bf8477b89193addcba25bd4fa13ad4df82612d60871edaa2"} {"question" "q-114063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/assets/9ac6bf6978169aaa3a29c570234cdbf26f37b6e91488d8e27c63255b8cbb3ba6.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={f}^{″}(x),$ where the function $f(x)$ is defined on $[0,3].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave down." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/state.json" "file_sha256" "9853dfd80c511e06bf8477b89193addcba25bd4fa13ad4df82612d60871edaa2"} {"question" "q-114063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A function $f(x)$ is concave down over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={f}^{″}(x),$ we can see that ${f}^{″}(x)<0$ on the interval  $(1,2)$ only. Therefore, the function $f(x)$ is concave down on the interval $(1,2)$ only." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/state.json" "file_sha256" "9853dfd80c511e06bf8477b89193addcba25bd4fa13ad4df82612d60871edaa2"} {"question" "q-114063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ce03f4f75f1275c5c0ab23092d155e04e8161764ce97c90f3fc6d28c8e8dede"} {"question" "q-114063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/state.json" "file_sha256" "9853dfd80c511e06bf8477b89193addcba25bd4fa13ad4df82612d60871edaa2"} {"question" "q-114063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(0,1)$ and $(1,2)$"] ["text" "$(0,1)$ and $(2,3)$"] ["text" "$(0,1)$ only"] ["text" "$(1,2)$ only"] ["text" "$(2,3)$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/state.json" "file_sha256" "9853dfd80c511e06bf8477b89193addcba25bd4fa13ad4df82612d60871edaa2"} {"question" "q-114063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(1,2)$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/state.json" "file_sha256" "9853dfd80c511e06bf8477b89193addcba25bd4fa13ad4df82612d60871edaa2"} {"question" "q-114273" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}(5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x),$ defined on $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0,e)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,e)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(e,\\infty )$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/state.json" "file_sha256" "9853dfd80c511e06bf8477b89193addcba25bd4fa13ad4df82612d60871edaa2"} {"question" "q-114273" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x(5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{x} \\\\  & =2x(5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-2x \\\\  & =2x(4-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2(4-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-2x\\cdot \\frac{2}{x} \\\\  & =8-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-4 \\\\  & =4(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & >0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & <1 \\\\ x & <e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on  $(0,e).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $4(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)<0,$ which has the solution $(e,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/state.json" "file_sha256" "9853dfd80c511e06bf8477b89193addcba25bd4fa13ad4df82612d60871edaa2"} {"question" "q-114273" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ce03f4f75f1275c5c0ab23092d155e04e8161764ce97c90f3fc6d28c8e8dede"} {"question" "q-114273" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/state.json" "file_sha256" "9853dfd80c511e06bf8477b89193addcba25bd4fa13ad4df82612d60871edaa2"} {"question" "q-114273" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/state.json" "file_sha256" "9853dfd80c511e06bf8477b89193addcba25bd4fa13ad4df82612d60871edaa2"} {"question" "q-114273" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/state.json" "file_sha256" "9853dfd80c511e06bf8477b89193addcba25bd4fa13ad4df82612d60871edaa2"} {"question" "q-344765" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/assets/56265e4a47a878233f27b1e265158dc766aa410144dfe95a20ce7f672b797802.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\nThe graph of the second derivative $y={f}^{″}(x)$ of the function $f(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the points $x=-4$ and $x=1,$ as shown above. Find the interval on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up.\n\n$f(x)$ is concave up on the interval\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/state.json" "file_sha256" "9853dfd80c511e06bf8477b89193addcba25bd4fa13ad4df82612d60871edaa2"} {"question" "q-344765" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A function $f(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFrom the plotted graph of $y={f}^{″}(x),$ we can see that ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ on the interval $(-4,1).$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval $(\\,-4,1\\,).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/state.json" "file_sha256" "9853dfd80c511e06bf8477b89193addcba25bd4fa13ad4df82612d60871edaa2"} {"question" "q-344765" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ce03f4f75f1275c5c0ab23092d155e04e8161764ce97c90f3fc6d28c8e8dede"} {"question" "q-344765" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/state.json" "file_sha256" "9853dfd80c511e06bf8477b89193addcba25bd4fa13ad4df82612d60871edaa2"} {"question" "q-344765" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/state.json" "file_sha256" "9853dfd80c511e06bf8477b89193addcba25bd4fa13ad4df82612d60871edaa2"} {"question" "q-344765" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/state.json" "file_sha256" "9853dfd80c511e06bf8477b89193addcba25bd4fa13ad4df82612d60871edaa2"} {"question" "q-344765" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/state.json" "file_sha256" "9